module

import Mathlib

/-!
# Ball quotients and complex hyperbolic lattices: target signatures

**This file is not the roadmap.** The roadmap is `README.md`. This file states the roadmap's
headline theorems that are statable against the pinned Mathlib and Tau Ceti, each with `sorry`,
together with the few definitions they need: the indefinite unitary group `U(1,n)`
(`indefiniteUnitaryGroup`), `PU(1,n)` as its quotient by the centre (`projectiveUnitaryGroup`),
the fractional linear action on the unit ball `B` of `ℂⁿ` and its boundary sphere (`ballMap`),
the distance of the invariant Kähler metric in closed form (`ballDist`), parabolic elements and
cusp points (`IsParabolic`, `IsCuspPoint`), lattices (`IsLattice`), the conjugation
representation (`conjugationMap`) and neat subgroups (`IsNeat`). The Hermitian form, the groups
and the ball belong to RealSemisimpleLieGroups §5.7 and lattices in locally compact groups to
VonNeumannAlgebras Layer 9; neither carrier is at the pins, so the definitions here are the
smallest ones the statements need, each with a real body. The statements hold for every `n ≥ 0`;
for `n = 0` the ball is a point and `PU(1,0)` is trivial.

Stated here: `PU(1,n)` is the biholomorphism group of the ball (README §2.1), the Margulis lemma
for discrete subgroups of `PU(1,n)` (§4.4), the finiteness of the cusp classes of a lattice and
the criterion that a lattice is cocompact exactly when it has no cusp point (§6.1), the finite
presentation of lattices (§6.2), Selberg's lemma in eigenvalue form, for the group generated by
the eigenvalues (§6.3), and the existence of normal neat subgroups of finite index (§6.4).

Not statable at the pins, for the reason given:
* the classification of totally geodesic complex subvarieties of the ball as sub-balls (§3.3):
  neither library has complex-analytic subsets of a complex manifold (irreducible components,
  regular locus), and the invariant metric of the ball is not a declared Riemannian metric;
* `Γ\B` as a normal complex analytic space (§7.1): no complex analytic spaces;
* the toroidal compactification of a neat quotient, with boundary components abelian varieties
  (§8.2): no analytification of projective varieties, so "abelian variety" cannot be said of a
  complex torus;
* Mok's theorem, that the minimal compactification `S*_Γ` is a normal projective variety with a
  finite boundary (§§9.3–9.5), the extension theorem into `S*_Γ` (§10.2), Borel algebraicity
  (§10.3) and the uniqueness of the algebraic model and of the finite-point normal
  compactification (§§10.4–10.5): no complex analytic spaces and no analytification functor from
  varieties over `ℂ`;
* the Shimura datum of an arithmetic lattice and the comparison of `S*_Γ` with the Baily–Borel
  compactification (§§11.3–11.4): no pure Shimura data and no Baily–Borel compactification;
* Γ-special subvarieties are exactly the totally geodesic ones (§§12.2–12.3), and the intersection
  inequality for algebraic subvarieties of `B × S_Γ` (§12.4): no analytification and no analytic
  subsets.
-/

namespace TauCetiRoadmap.BallQuotientsAndComplexHyperbolicLattices

open Matrix MeasureTheory Metric Set

/-! ## Layer 1: The group PU(1,n) and the ball -/

/-- The indefinite unitary group `U(1,n)`: the invertible matrices `g` on `ℂ^{Fin 1 ⊕ Fin n}` with
`gᴴ J g = J`, where `J = diag(1, −1, …, −1)` is Mathlib's
`LieAlgebra.Orthogonal.indefiniteDiagonal (Fin 1) (Fin n) ℂ`, the Gram matrix of the Hermitian
form `⟨z, w⟩ = conj(z₀) w₀ − Σ conj(zᵢ) wᵢ` of signature `(1,n)` (README Conventions and §1.1;
RealSemisimpleLieGroups §5.7). -/
def indefiniteUnitaryGroup (n : ℕ) : Subgroup (GL (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin n) ℂ) where
  carrier := {g | star g.val * LieAlgebra.Orthogonal.indefiniteDiagonal (Fin 1) (Fin n) ℂ * g.val =
      LieAlgebra.Orthogonal.indefiniteDiagonal (Fin 1) (Fin n) ℂ}
  mul_mem' := by
    intro a b ha hb
    simp only [Set.mem_ofPred_eq, Units.val_mul, star_mul] at *
    rw [show star b.val * star a.val * LieAlgebra.Orthogonal.indefiniteDiagonal (Fin 1) (Fin n) ℂ *
        (a.val * b.val) = star b.val * (star a.val *
          LieAlgebra.Orthogonal.indefiniteDiagonal (Fin 1) (Fin n) ℂ * a.val) * b.val by
      simp only [Matrix.mul_assoc], ha, hb]
  one_mem' := by simp
  inv_mem' := by
    intro a ha
    simp only [Set.mem_ofPred_eq] at *
    conv_lhs => rw [← ha]
    rw [show star (a⁻¹).val * (star a.val *
        LieAlgebra.Orthogonal.indefiniteDiagonal (Fin 1) (Fin n) ℂ * a.val) * (a⁻¹).val =
        star (a.val * (a⁻¹).val) * LieAlgebra.Orthogonal.indefiniteDiagonal (Fin 1) (Fin n) ℂ *
          (a.val * (a⁻¹).val) by
      simp only [star_mul, Matrix.mul_assoc]]
    simp

/-- `PU(1,n) = U(1,n)/U(1)`: the quotient of `U(1,n)` by its centre, which is the group of unit
scalars, with the quotient topology (README §§1.1–1.2). -/
abbrev projectiveUnitaryGroup (n : ℕ) : Type :=
  indefiniteUnitaryGroup n ⧸ Subgroup.center (indefiniteUnitaryGroup n)

/-- The fractional linear map `w ↦ (c + D w)/(a + bᵀ w)` of a matrix `g = [[a, bᵀ], [c, D]]`
(blocks of sizes `1` and `n`): the line `[g (1, w)]` read in the affine chart `z₀ ≠ 0`. For
`g ∈ U(1,n)` the denominator does not vanish on the closed unit ball, and the map is the action
of `g` on the bounded model `B` of the ball and on its boundary sphere; scalar matrices act
trivially (README Conventions and §1.4). -/
noncomputable def ballMap {n : ℕ} (g : GL (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin n) ℂ) (w : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)) :
    EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n) :=
  let z := g.val *ᵥ Sum.elim (fun _ => 1) (fun i => w i)
  WithLp.toLp 2 fun i => z (Sum.inr i) / z (Sum.inl 0)

/-! ## Layer 2: Biholomorphisms and the invariant Kähler metric -/

/-- **`PU(1,n)` is the biholomorphism group of the ball** (README §2.1, through H. Cartan's
uniqueness theorem). A map `f` of `ℂⁿ` restricts to a biholomorphism of the open unit ball `B`
exactly when it agrees on `B` with the fractional linear map of an element of `U(1,n)`, and the
class of that element in `PU(1,n)` is unique. -/
theorem biholomorphic_ball_iff (n : ℕ)
    (f : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n) → EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)) :
    (MapsTo f (ball 0 1) (ball 0 1) ∧ DifferentiableOn ℂ f (ball 0 1) ∧
      ∃ f' : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n) → EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n),
        MapsTo f' (ball 0 1) (ball 0 1) ∧ DifferentiableOn ℂ f' (ball 0 1) ∧
          LeftInvOn f' f (ball 0 1) ∧ RightInvOn f' f (ball 0 1)) ↔
      ∃! γ : projectiveUnitaryGroup n, ∃ g : indefiniteUnitaryGroup n,
        (g : projectiveUnitaryGroup n) = γ ∧ EqOn f (ballMap g) (ball 0 1) := by
  sorry

/-- The distance of the invariant Kähler metric `ω = −i∂∂̄ log(1 − ‖w‖²)` on the ball, in closed
form: `cosh²(d(w, w′)/√2) = |1 − ⟪w, w′⟫|² / ((1 − ‖w‖²)(1 − ‖w′‖²))`, the formula
`cosh²(d/√2) = |⟨z, z′⟩|²/(⟨z, z⟩⟨z′, z′⟩)` for `z = (1, w)`, `z′ = (1, w′)` (README §2.4, which
proves that it is the Riemannian distance of `ω`). The argument of `arcosh` is at least `1` on
`B`. -/
noncomputable def ballDist {n : ℕ} (w w' : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)) : ℝ :=
  √2 * Real.arcosh (‖1 - inner ℂ w w'‖ / √((1 - ‖w‖ ^ 2) * (1 - ‖w'‖ ^ 2)))

/-! ## Layer 4: Isometries, horoballs and the Margulis lemma -/

/-- `g` is parabolic: its fractional linear map has no fixed point in the open unit ball and
exactly one fixed point on the boundary sphere (README §4.1). -/
def IsParabolic {n : ℕ} (g : GL (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin n) ℂ) : Prop :=
  (∀ w ∈ ball (0 : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)) 1, ballMap g w ≠ w) ∧
    ∃! b, b ∈ sphere (0 : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)) 1 ∧ ballMap g b = b

/-- **The Margulis lemma for the ball** (README §4.4). There are `ε > 0` and `m` such that, for
every discrete subgroup `Δ` of `PU(1,n)` and every point `x` of the ball, the subgroup generated
by the elements of `Δ` that move `x` by less than `ε` has a nilpotent subgroup `N` of finite index
at most `m`. -/
theorem margulis_lemma (n : ℕ) :
    ∃ ε > 0, ∃ m : ℕ, ∀ Δ : Subgroup (projectiveUnitaryGroup n), DiscreteTopology Δ →
      ∀ x ∈ ball (0 : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)) 1,
        ∃ N : Subgroup (Subgroup.closure {γ | γ ∈ Δ ∧ ∃ g : indefiniteUnitaryGroup n,
          (g : projectiveUnitaryGroup n) = γ ∧ ballDist x (ballMap g x) < ε}),
          Group.IsNilpotent N ∧ N.FiniteIndex ∧ N.index ≤ m := by
  sorry

/-! ## Layer 5: Lattices in PU(1,n) -/

/-- A lattice in `PU(1,n)`: a discrete subgroup `Γ` with a fundamental domain of finite measure,
for a Haar measure, for its action on `PU(1,n)` by left multiplication. Inversion `g ↦ g⁻¹` turns
such a domain into one of the same measure for the right action used by VonNeumannAlgebras
Layer 9, `PU(1,n)` being unimodular (README §5.1). -/
def IsLattice {n : ℕ} (Γ : Subgroup (projectiveUnitaryGroup n)) : Prop :=
  DiscreteTopology Γ ∧ ∃ μ : Measure (projectiveUnitaryGroup n), μ.IsHaarMeasure ∧
    ∃ s : Set (projectiveUnitaryGroup n), IsFundamentalDomain Γ s μ ∧ μ s ≠ ⊤

/-! ## Layer 6: Cusps, finite generation and Selberg's lemma -/

/-- A cusp point of a subgroup `Γ` of `PU(1,n)`: a point of the boundary sphere fixed by a
parabolic element of `Γ` (README §6.1). -/
def IsCuspPoint {n : ℕ} (Γ : Subgroup (projectiveUnitaryGroup n))
    (b : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)) : Prop :=
  b ∈ sphere (0 : EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)) 1 ∧ ∃ g : indefiniteUnitaryGroup n,
    (g : projectiveUnitaryGroup n) ∈ Γ ∧ IsParabolic (g : GL (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin n) ℂ) ∧ ballMap g b = b

/-- **Finitely many cusp classes** (README §6.1, the cusp structure theorem for every lattice,
after Garland–Raghunathan). The cusp points of a lattice `Γ` of `PU(1,n)` lie in finitely many
`Γ`-orbits. -/
theorem IsLattice.exists_finset_cuspPoint_orbits {n : ℕ}
    {Γ : Subgroup (projectiveUnitaryGroup n)} (hΓ : IsLattice Γ) :
    ∃ F : Finset (EuclideanSpace ℂ (Fin n)), ∀ b, IsCuspPoint Γ b →
      ∃ c ∈ F, ∃ g : indefiniteUnitaryGroup n, (g : projectiveUnitaryGroup n) ∈ Γ ∧
        ballMap g c = b := by
  sorry

/-- **The cocompactness criterion** (README §6.1). A lattice `Γ` of `PU(1,n)` is cocompact,
the space `PU(1,n)/Γ` of left cosets `gΓ` being compact (equivalently `Γ\PU(1,n)`, through
`g ↦ g⁻¹`), exactly when it has no cusp point. -/
theorem IsLattice.compactSpace_quotient_iff {n : ℕ}
    {Γ : Subgroup (projectiveUnitaryGroup n)} (hΓ : IsLattice Γ) :
    CompactSpace (projectiveUnitaryGroup n ⧸ Γ) ↔ ∀ b, ¬ IsCuspPoint Γ b := by
  sorry

/-- **Lattices are finitely presented** (README §6.2). -/
theorem IsLattice.isFinitelyPresented {n : ℕ} {Γ : Subgroup (projectiveUnitaryGroup n)}
    (hΓ : IsLattice Γ) : Group.IsFinitelyPresented Γ := by
  sorry

/-- **Selberg's lemma in eigenvalue form** (README §6.3). For a field `F` and a finite index set
`ι`, a finitely generated subgroup `Λ` of `GL_ι(F)` has a normal subgroup `Λ'` of finite index
such that, for every `g ∈ Λ'`, the subgroup of `(AlgebraicClosure F)ˣ` generated by the
eigenvalues of `g` (the roots of its characteristic polynomial) is torsion-free; in particular no
eigenvalue of an element of `Λ'` is a nontrivial root of unity. In characteristic `0`, `Λ'` is
torsion-free; in characteristic `p` it may contain unipotent elements of order `p`
(Borel, *Introduction aux groupes arithmétiques*, Cor. 17.7; Witte Morris, *Introduction to
Arithmetic Groups*, Remark 4.8.5(2)). -/
theorem selberg_lemma {F : Type*} [Field F] {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι]
    [DecidableEq ι] (Λ : Subgroup (GL ι F)) (hΛ : Group.FG Λ) :
    ∃ Λ' ≤ Λ, (Λ'.subgroupOf Λ).Normal ∧ (Λ'.subgroupOf Λ).FiniteIndex ∧
      ∀ g ∈ Λ', ∀ ζ ∈ Subgroup.closure {u : (AlgebraicClosure F)ˣ |
          (g.val.charpoly.map (algebraMap F (AlgebraicClosure F))).IsRoot (u : AlgebraicClosure F)},
        IsOfFinOrder ζ → ζ = 1 := by
  sorry

/-- The conjugation representation `X ↦ g X g⁻¹` of `GL_ι(ℂ)` on the matrices `M_ι(ℂ)`; on
`PU(1,n)` it is the representation `Ad` of README §1.2, computed on any lift. -/
noncomputable def conjugationMap {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι] (g : GL ι ℂ) :
    Module.End ℂ (Matrix ι ι ℂ) :=
  LinearMap.mulLeft ℂ g.val ∘ₗ LinearMap.mulRight ℂ (g⁻¹).val

/-- A subgroup `Γ` of `PU(1,n)` is neat when, for every element of `Γ`, the eigenvalues of its
conjugation representation on `M_{n+1}(ℂ)`, computed on any lift to `U(1,n)`, generate a
torsion-free subgroup of `ℂˣ` (README Conventions and §6.4; the condition called net in Witte
Morris, *Introduction to Arithmetic Groups*, Remark 4.8.5(2)). -/
def IsNeat {n : ℕ} (Γ : Subgroup (projectiveUnitaryGroup n)) : Prop :=
  ∀ g : indefiniteUnitaryGroup n, (g : projectiveUnitaryGroup n) ∈ Γ →
    ∀ ζ ∈ Subgroup.closure
        {u : ℂˣ | (conjugationMap (g : GL (Fin 1 ⊕ Fin n) ℂ)).HasEigenvalue (u : ℂ)},
      IsOfFinOrder ζ → ζ = 1

/-- **Neat subgroups of finite index** (README §6.4). Every lattice of `PU(1,n)` has a normal
neat subgroup of finite index. -/
theorem IsLattice.exists_neat {n : ℕ} {Γ : Subgroup (projectiveUnitaryGroup n)}
    (hΓ : IsLattice Γ) :
    ∃ Γ' ≤ Γ, (Γ'.subgroupOf Γ).Normal ∧ (Γ'.subgroupOf Γ).FiniteIndex ∧ IsNeat Γ' := by
  sorry

end TauCetiRoadmap.BallQuotientsAndComplexHyperbolicLattices
